title: Log endpoints
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: GET /log (bounded two ways), the change feed GET /log/since, one label in detail, and opening and reading labels.

|!Route |!Does |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/log">> |the history, as operations and the labels they are in (below) |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/log/since">> `?op=<id>` |the change feed: the newest operation, and every operation created after `op` |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/log/labels/:id">> |one label with its own operations and their snapshots; `head` for the newest operation's |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/labels">> |opens a label (below) |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/labels/:id">> |one label, its ancestors and its children |

Every write request may name the label it writes in with the header
`X-Metafolder-Label: <id>` ([[Labels and contexts]]); without one, the write opens a label
of its own, named after the route (`PUT /repos/:repo/metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name`). A
header that is not a number is a `400`; one naming a label that does not exist, or the
watcher's or one inside it, is a `400` too, and nothing is written.

A request that is one step of a client's command says so: `X-Metafolder-Command: <32 hex
digits>`, a key the client draws for the command, with `X-Metafolder-Command-Name` and
`X-Metafolder-Command-Description` (percent-encoded UTF-8). Its first write opens the
command's label — inside `X-Metafolder-Label`'s when there is one — and the later writes
with the same key go in it; a read opens nothing. A key that is not 32 hex digits is a
`400`.

!! GET /log

|!Parameter |!Default |!Does |
|`metarecord_uuid` |— |only the operations of that metarecord |
|`limit` |— |at most this many operations, the newest |
|`labels` |— |at most this many labels (each operation counted in its innermost one), the newest — whole unless `limit` cuts the oldest |
|`since`, `until` |— |only operations written within (Unix ms) |
|`include_snapshots` |`false` |each operation with `snapshots_before`/`snapshots_after` |

```
{"head": 3, "first": 1,
 "operations": [{"id": 1, "label_id": 1, "timestamp": 1700000000000, "origin": null,
                 "op_type": "create_metarecord", "entity_uuid": "…", "field_name": null,
                 "reverts_op_id": null}, …],
 "labels": [{"id": 1, "name": "POST /repos/init", "description": null, "parent": null,
             "created": 1700000000000}, …],
 "total_operations": 3, "total_labels": 2}
```

Operations run oldest first — the log is a sequence ([[The operation log]]). `head` is the
newest operation, `first` the oldest still in the log — where undo stops ([[The undo selection]]). `origin` is `"watcher"` for what the daemon recorded for the filesystem,
`"trash"` for a trashing, `null` otherwise ([[Operations and their origin]]). `labels` lists
the labels the returned operations are in ''and all their ancestors'', so a client can
draw the nesting; a label whose own operations the `limit` cut carries `"partial": true`
(how many it holds in all is not counted: that would read it whole). The totals describe
the ''whole'' log, whatever the window. Bounds limit the read itself, and a filter widens it
until it finds what was asked ([[Reading the log costs the window]]).

!! GET /log/since

The change feed clients poll: the current `head` and every operation created after `op`,
each naming its `entity_uuid`. Without `op`, just the head. The log only grows, so `head`
only moves forward. A delta larger than `limit` (default 500) comes back `truncated` and
empty, and a client falls back to a full refresh. The GUI's change feed is built on it.

!! One label

`GET /log/labels/:id` always includes snapshots (it is for the detail view):
`{"label": {"id", "name", "description", "parent", "created", "ancestors", "children",
"is_head", "operation_count"}, "operations": [{"id", "label_id", "timestamp", "origin",
"op_type", "entity_uuid", "field_name", "snapshots_before", "snapshots_after"}, …]}`, a
snapshot being a row in column form
(`{"field_id": 43, "field_name": "rating", "value_type": "int", "value_int": 3}`). Only
the label's ''own'' operations are listed — its children are labels of their own. A label
may hold a whole reconcile, so `offset`/`limit` window its operations and `op` picks one
(`404` if not in it); `is_head` and `operation_count` always describe the whole label.
`404` for an unknown label.

!! Opening a label

`POST /repos/:repo/labels` with `{"name": "tag run", "description": "…", "parent": 12}`
(description and parent optional) answers the label as `GET /labels/:id` does. Without
`parent`, the label is a child of the one the request names in `X-Metafolder-Label`, or a
root. An empty name, or a parent that does not exist or is the watcher's, is a `400`. A
label opened this way and never written in stays, empty; one a write opened is dropped with
it when the write records nothing.

`GET /repos/:repo/labels/:id` answers `{"id", "name", "description", "parent", "created",
"ancestors": [innermost first], "children": [ids]}`, `404` for an unknown label.
